<!-- 6. REGULATORY COMPLIANCE, TESTING, AND SAFETY STANDARDS -->
<section class="section" id="6-regulatory-compliance-testing-and-safety-standards">
  <h2>6. Regulatory Compliance, Testing, and Safety Standards</h2>
  <p>Sunscreen aerosols are classified as over-the-counter (OTC) drugs or regulated cosmetics, requiring strict SPF and safety audits. In India, the CDSCO monitors ingredient safety, while global markets require FDA or European registrations. Formulators must also conduct droplet size testing to ensure particles remain above 10 microns. The primary testing requirements include:</p>
  <p>Launching a sunscreen spray requires extensive clinical and analytical testing to verify its safety, efficacy, and container durability. Because sunscreens are classified as over-the-counter (OTC) drugs in some regions (like the USA) and as cosmetics in others (like Europe and India), compliance is highly dependent on target market rules. Founders must plan and budget for these mandatory testing phases, which directly impact the product launch timeline and marketing claims.</p>

            <h3>Determining SPF, Broad Spectrum, and Water Resistance</h3>
            <p>In-vivo SPF testing on human volunteers is required to verify the level of UVB protection, while in-vitro testing measures broad-spectrum UVA protection. For products claiming water resistance, volunteers are tested after specific periods of water immersion (typically 40 or 80 minutes) to confirm the sunscreen film remains protective. All SPF values printed on the final retail label must be supported by certified laboratory reports.</p>

            <h3>Inhalation Safety and Particle Size Distribution Guidelines</h3>
            <p>Because aerosol sprays generate airborne droplets, safety agencies require testing to minimize the risk of consumer lung inhalation. The actuator and formulation must be optimized to ensure that the bulk of the spray droplets have a median diameter greater than 30 microns. Droplets smaller than 10 microns are considered respirable and can penetrate deep into the lungs, representing a health hazard that regulators actively monitor during safety assessments.</p>

            <h3>Corrosion Testing, Shelf-Life Stability, and Compatibility Studies</h3>
            <p>Accelerated stability studies store the pressurized cans at elevated temperatures (typically 40°C) for 3 to 6 months to simulate a multi-year product shelf life. Chemists routinely open these cans to inspect the metal for microscopic pitting or corrosion and to check the internal plastic components for swelling. If the formula reacts with the metal container, it can result in can leakage, discoloration of the product, or degradation of the active UV filters.</p>
        </section>

